# JobDigest categorisation watchdog — alerts when the corpus stops being classified.
#
# The third watchdog, one stage apart from the other two. jobdigest-watchdog asks whether a
# subscriber got a digest; jobdigest-source-watchdog asks whether each source still delivers;
# this asks whether what they deliver is still being *understood*.
#
# `role_category` decides what enters a shortlist. When it degrades nothing raises: no
# exception, no failed timer, no changed row count — the subscriber's shortlist just gets
# thinner, which is indistinguishable from a quiet week.
#
# Both obvious metrics were measured against production and rejected before this was written
# (see service/categorization_watchdog.py): per-source day-over-day swings 47 points on
# healthy sources, and corpus-wide-over-new-rows swings 14.9% -> 59.7% on source mix alone.
# Drift is therefore judged over the whole active corpus against stored history
# (migration 017), and it stays silent until it has 4 days of it.
#
# Install:
#   sudo cp deploy/jobdigest-categorization-watchdog.* /etc/systemd/system/
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl enable --now jobdigest-categorization-watchdog.timer
[Unit]
Description=JobDigest categorisation watchdog (the corpus is still being classified)
After=docker.service
Requires=docker.service
# The alert IS the non-zero exit: jobdigest-alert.py mails the last journal lines. Same
# single notification path as the other two watchdogs.
OnFailure=jobdigest-alert@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=/opt/jobdigest/deploy
# Not --quiet: unlike the source watchdog, this one records a daily snapshot as a side effect,
# so the one-line corpus summary is the receipt that the snapshot was taken. Without it a
# silently failing recorder looks identical to a healthy day.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/docker compose run --rm pipeline python -m service.categorization_watchdog
